---
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';
import { breadcrumbLd } from '@/lib/seo';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, COMPANY_ADDRESS } from '@/lib/claims';
import { TAG_IDS, TAG_LABELS, anyTagConfigured } from '@/lib/tags';
const bcLd = breadcrumbLd([{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'Privacy policy' }]);

/** Build-time date for "Last updated" line. */
/** The page describes the build it ships with: the cookie sections appear only when a tag is switched on. */
const tagsOn = anyTagConfigured(TAG_IDS);
const description = tagsOn
  ? 'What we store when you browse, use the free tools, request a quote, order or review, and how to switch analytics and marketing cookies on or off.'
  : 'No tracking cookies, no ads; one human check on forms. Exactly what we store when you browse, use the free tools, request a quote, order or review.';
const lastUpdated = new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10);
---
<Base title="Privacy policy" description={description} jsonLd={bcLd}>
  <div class="container-x py-10 md:py-16">
    <div class="prose-calib mx-auto flex max-w-[720px] flex-col gap-5">
      <h1 class="h-display m-0 text-[32px] md:text-[40px]">Privacy policy</h1>

      {tagsOn ? (
        <p><strong>In short:</strong> we use analytics and marketing cookies, and you can switch them off at any time under &ldquo;Privacy settings&rdquo; at the bottom of every page. Beyond that we only keep what we need to answer you, ship your order and run the site.</p>
      ) : (
        <p><strong>In short:</strong> we don&rsquo;t track you, set no advertising or analytics cookies and load no third-party scripts except Cloudflare&rsquo;s human check on forms. We only keep what we need to answer you, ship your order and run the site.</p>
      )}

      <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
      <p>
        Calib.Tools LLC, {COMPANY_ADDRESS} &mdash; <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Browsing the site</h2>
      <ul class="ml-5 flex flex-col gap-2 text-[15px] list-disc">
        <li><strong>Hosting.</strong> The site runs on Cloudflare Pages. Cloudflare processes your IP address and browser details to deliver pages and block attacks. See <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloudflare&rsquo;s privacy policy</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Page counts.</strong> We count page views on the server: page path and a referrer category such as &ldquo;generator&rdquo; or &ldquo;guide&rdquo;. No IP addresses, visitor IDs or sessions are stored, so a count can&rsquo;t be traced back to you. Known crawlers are skipped; the User-Agent is read for that check and not stored.</li>
        <li><strong>In your browser.</strong> We store a cart ID on your device (<code>localStorage</code> key <code>calib-cart-id</code>), created when you first add something to the cart. After you send a quote request, a summary of it is kept for that browser tab only (<code>sessionStorage</code> key <code>ct-quote</code>) so the confirmation page can show it; it is gone when you close the tab. No cookies unless you sign in (see Customer accounts).</li>
        <li><strong>Fonts.</strong> Geist fonts are served from our own domain; no external font services.</li>
      </ul>

      {tagsOn && (
        <>
          <h2>Analytics and marketing cookies</h2>
          <p><strong>Your choice.</strong> These cookies are on by default. The first time you visit, a bar at the bottom of the page says so: &ldquo;Accept&rdquo; keeps them on, and &ldquo;Options&rdquo; lets you turn everything off or pick categories.</p>
          <p>If your browser sends a Global Privacy Control signal, they stay off. Visitors in the United States also see &ldquo;Do not sell or share my personal information&rdquo;, which switches them off in one step.</p>
          <p>To decide whether to show that link, our server reads the two-letter country code Cloudflare attaches to your request; your IP address is not stored. Your choice is kept on your device (<code>localStorage</code> key <code>ct-consent</code>).</p>
          <p>Once you have made a choice, it is passed to our checkout on shop.calib.tools, which Shopify runs, so the same choice applies there. For this Shopify sets the cookie <code>_tracking_consent</code>.</p>
          <ul class="ml-5 flex flex-col gap-2 text-[15px] list-disc">
            {TAG_IDS.ga4 && <li><strong>{TAG_LABELS.ga4}</strong> (analytics). Google Analytics 4 shows us which pages are visited, roughly where visitors are (country and city), the kind of device and the site they came from. It also records that a quote request, an add to cart, a file download or a saved pattern happened, not what you entered. It sets the cookies <code>_ga</code> and <code>_ga_*</code>, which last up to two years, and Google processes the data on its servers, including in the United States. See <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google&rsquo;s privacy policy</a>.</li>}
            {TAG_IDS.googleAds && <li><strong>{TAG_LABELS.googleAds}</strong> (marketing). Measures whether a visit that came from one of our ads led to a quote or an order, and lets us show our ads to people who visited. See <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/ads" target="_blank" rel="noopener">how Google uses advertising cookies</a>.</li>}
            {TAG_IDS.linkedin && <li><strong>{TAG_LABELS.linkedin}</strong> (marketing). Measures visits that came from LinkedIn and lets us show our posts and ads to people who visited. See <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/legal/privacy-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn&rsquo;s privacy policy</a>.</li>}
            {TAG_IDS.hubspot && <li><strong>{TAG_LABELS.hubspot}</strong> (marketing). Links your visits to the quote requests and emails you send us, so the person answering you sees the context. While marketing is on, what you enter in a form that asks for your email address (quote request, contact, newsletter) is also passed to HubSpot. It sets the cookies <code>hubspotutk</code>, <code>__hstc</code>, <code>__hssc</code> and <code>__hssrc</code>, which are removed when you switch marketing off. HubSpot stores this data in the United States. See <a href="https://legal.hubspot.com/privacy-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HubSpot&rsquo;s privacy policy</a>.</li>}
            {TAG_IDS.meta && <li><strong>{TAG_LABELS.meta}</strong> (marketing). Measures whether a visit that came from one of our ads on Facebook or Instagram led to a product view, an add to cart or a start of checkout, and lets us show our ads to people who visited. Meta receives the page address, the product number and the amount, not your name, email address or anything you type. It sets the cookies <code>_fbp</code> and <code>_fbc</code>, which are removed when you switch marketing off. See <a href="https://www.facebook.com/privacy/policy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Meta&rsquo;s privacy policy</a>.</li>}
          </ul>
        </>
      )}

      <h2>Free tools</h2>
      <p>Everything you enter in the generator, calculators and image checks stays in your browser. Images you check are not uploaded.</p>

      <h2>Customer accounts</h2>
      <p>Signing in is optional. Sign-in runs through Shopify customer accounts, on a Shopify page. You can enter your email and receive a one-time code, or continue with your Google account or with Shop; there is no password. Your account, quotes and orders are kept by Shopify.</p>
      <p>If you continue with Google, Google tells Shopify your email address and nothing else.</p>
      <p>Signing in uses two first-party cookies on calib.tools. Both are HttpOnly (page scripts cannot read them) and neither is used for tracking:</p>
      <ul class="ml-5 flex flex-col gap-2 text-[15px] list-disc">
        <li><code>ct_login</code>: set when you click Sign in and deleted when you come back from Shopify, at most 10 minutes. It holds a random value that makes sure sign-in completes in the browser that started it.</li>
        <li><code>ct_session</code>: set after you sign in. It holds the encrypted Shopify sign-in tokens that keep you signed in for up to 14 days and is deleted when you sign out.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Patterns you save from the generator are stored on your Shopify customer record as a list of generator links with the names you give them. You can remove them on your account page at any time.</p>

      <h2>Quote requests</h2>
      <p>When you send the quote form, we receive your name, company, work email, product, material, quantity, delivery country, message and, if you came from the generator, the link to your pattern.</p>
      <p>Each request gets a reference number. It arrives by email (sent through Resend) and a copy is kept in our Cloudflare storage so no request is lost.</p>
      <p>A custom quotation is emailed to the address on the offer, also through Resend, with the quotation attached as a PDF.</p>
      <p>We send you one confirmation email with that number and a link to confirm your address; clicking the link only records that the address is yours.</p>
      <p>Your IP address is used for one hour to limit spam. We use all of this only to answer and follow up on your request.</p>
      <p>To check that a work address can receive mail, our server looks up the mail server of its domain (not the address itself) through Cloudflare DNS.</p>

      <h2>Human check on forms</h2>
      <p>The quote, newsletter and review forms use Cloudflare Turnstile to tell people from bots. Turnstile runs a short check in your browser and sends the result to Cloudflare; it does not use advertising cookies. See <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/turnstile-privacy-policy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloudflare&rsquo;s Turnstile privacy notice</a>.</p>

      <h2>Orders</h2>
      <p>Checkout runs on Shopify. Shopify processes your name, address, email and payment details to take payment and ship your order; we never see your full card number. We share your address with the carrier that delivers it. See <a href="https://www.shopify.com/legal/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shopify&rsquo;s privacy policy</a>.</p>

      <h2>Review invitations</h2>
      <p>After your order ships, we store your name, email and the product you bought for up to 30 days so we can invite you to review it. If you write a review, we publish your name as you entered it and your text.</p>

      <h2>Newsletter</h2>
      <p>If you subscribe, Shopify stores your email address. Every email has an unsubscribe link.</p>

      <h2>Release notification</h2>
      <p>If you ask to be told when Plumbline is out, Shopify stores your email address with a note of that request. We use it for that one email. It does not subscribe you to the newsletter.</p>

      <h2>How long we keep data</h2>
      <p>Quote and order emails are kept as long as we need them for the request, the order, warranty and tax records. Review invitations expire after 30 days. Ask us any time to delete data we don&rsquo;t need to keep by law.</p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>You can ask what we hold about you, correct it, delete it, take a copy or object to how we use it. Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. If you&rsquo;re in the EU or UK, you can also complain to your local data protection authority.</p>
      {tagsOn ? (
        <p>California residents: &ldquo;Do not sell or share my personal information&rdquo; under &ldquo;Privacy settings&rdquo; switches off analytics and marketing cookies. We honor Global Privacy Control.</p>
      ) : (
        <p>California residents: we don&rsquo;t sell or share personal information for advertising.</p>
      )}

      <h2>Legal basis (EU/UK)</h2>
      <p>Order and quote data: to perform a contract or take steps toward one. Hosting, spam limits and page counts: our legitimate interest in running a secure site. Newsletter and release notification: your consent.</p>
      {tagsOn && <p>Analytics and marketing cookies: our interest in understanding how the site is used and in advertising. You can switch them off under &ldquo;Privacy settings&rdquo;.</p>}

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>We update this page when what we collect changes. Last updated: {lastUpdated}.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
